---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="For community pottery studios"
	description="Kilnbook for community and membership pottery studios: members book wheel time, log work for shared kilns and join classes, all without a whiteboard."
>
	<section class="band-dark page-hero">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">For community studios</p>
				<h1>A shared studio, without the shared spreadsheet.</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					Dozens of members, a handful of wheels, kilns everyone fires in, and open-studio hours that
					run whether or not you are there. Kilnbook keeps track of who booked what, whose work is in
					which load, and who still has classes left.
				</p>
				<a href="/contact" class="btn">Book a demo</a>
			</div>
			<img
				src="/images/studio-floor.svg"
				alt="A community studio floor plan with wheels, hand-building tables and kilns"
				width="480"
				height="309"
			/>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-alt">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">Sound familiar?</p>
			<h2>What makes a membership studio hard to run</h2>
			<div class="cards">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Everyone wants the same wheel</h3>
					<p>
						Evenings and weekends are full, the sign-up sheet gets crossed out, and members on the
						cheapest tier book more hours than members paying for unlimited.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Nobody knows what is on the kiln shelf</h3>
					<p>
						Unlabelled pots, firing fees counted by hand at the end of the month, and members asking
						every day whether their glaze load has come out yet.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Classes and members get tangled</h3>
					<p>
						Beginners in a six-week course, members dropping in, class packs tracked on paper, and a
						waitlist that lives in your inbox.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Wheel time members book themselves</h2>
		<p>
			Members reserve wheels, hand-building tables and shared equipment in one-hour blocks from their
			phone. You set how many hours each membership tier gets, how far ahead they can book, and
			whether peak hours count double. A tablet by the door handles open-studio check-in, so you
			always know who is in the building while the kilns are running.
			<a href="/features/wheel-time">How wheel time works</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Shared kilns with a queue everyone can see</h2>
		<p>
			Members log each piece when they set it on the bisque shelf. Kilnbook charges the firing fee by
			cubic inch or per piece, puts it in the right load for the right kiln, and texts the member
			when it is ready for pickup. Every kiln keeps its own firing log.
			<a href="/features/firings">How firing queues work</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Classes that bring in new members</h2>
		<p>
			Run six-week courses for beginners, drop-in sessions for members and class packs for everyone in
			between. When a class is full, the waitlist offers cancelled seats by text, and instructors see
			their roster from the studio tablet.
			<a href="/features/classes">How classes and packs work</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Which plan fits a community studio?</h2>
		<p>
			Most community studios run on the Studio plan at $79 a month: up to 3 kilns, unlimited members,
			wheel and table reservations, class packs, waitlists and memberships. Add a fourth kiln or a
			second location and the Collective plan has no kiln limit. A garage studio with one kiln can
			start on Starter. <a href="/pricing">Compare plans</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="cta-band">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Give your members the studio calendar.</h2>
			<p>30 days free on the Studio plan. No card to start, and nothing is deleted if you walk away.</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-dark">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
